 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Linear dresses, lightweight tailoring, sparkling Deco embroidery: at Lanvin, Peter Copping is deeply attuned to the life and work of Jeanne Lanvin (1867-1946). He has free rein to explore the library and archive of the couturier and entrepreneur, a pioneering woman ahead of her time in producing sportswear as well as dresses.</p><p>What he came up with for spring was another chapter in his evolving thoughts. Pinned to his research board were 30 mostly black-and-white images of women who inspire him now, and have done for a long time. “People always ask designers ‘Who is your woman,’ but I don’t have one,” he said. “I have all of these!”</p><p>Tina Chow, Anjelica Huston, Georgia O’Keeffe, Anna Piaggi, Elsa Peretti and <em>Vogue</em>’s own Tonne Goodman were among them. Each had evolved a personal style over her life which hardly deviated. It was the inspiration Copping needed to do what so many designers have turned to this season: stripping back and thinking of what the customer wants. “It has to be grounded in clothes that women can actually wear for different occasions,” he said.</p><p>From his opening cream, dropped-waist coat onwards, the daywear—soft adaptations of shawl-collared jackets merged with skirts, alternating with menswear-derived black and white tailoring—made absolute sense for a grown-up clientele. “I’ll always strip as much of the fabric away as I can, which I think is really important because basically, for any season, women can layer things up as they as they choose to,” said Copping. So it was that jackets and skirts tailored in soft silks—a Lanvin touch from the 1930s—became highly attractive propositions (especially two in tomato red) for surviving next year’s threatened heatwaves.</p><p>And then came the other loves of Copping’s life—his evening dresses. There was a beautiful, simple, drop-waisted pale charmeuse one that might have been lifted direct from Lanvin’s 1920s patterns. A geometrically embroidered tunic, and then a series of tie-on halter accessories, were developed from some Indian textiles Lanvin had collected on her many travels. “I liked the look of them when we shot them in black and white, to strip the color out,” Copping explained.</p><p>The tiny mirrors and the sequins shimmered. The fragile ’30s chiffon and lingerie lace dresses fluttered. As shown, near-nude, they’re for a younger customer for sure. Perhaps it had occurred to Copping that this is true to the mother-daughter market Jeanne Lanvin brought to her door in the first place.</p></div>",
